#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070021 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the point of intersection between the two lines\n\n$$\ny = x + 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = 2x + 1\n$$\n\nA sketch of both lines and the point of intersection $P$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bf429fdfbe706521b81617f56a0f561171aa7fd48e0af89318225f1d328e667.png)\n\nThe point $P$ consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=2x+1\n$$\n\nSo, to find the coordinates of $P$ we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n{y = x + 2; y = 2x + 1\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation\n\n$$\ny=2x+1\n$$\n\ngives an equation that we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2 &= 2x + 1 \\\\\nx + 1 &= 2x \\\\\n1 &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations, say\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x + 2 \\\\\n&= 1 + 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\nx=1, \\, y=3\n$$\n\nSo, the point of intersection has the coordinates $(1,3)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "39070934-9f3c-5858-8959-365242cf0563" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070034 :tutorial/content "When two lines are given in the *standard form*, you may find it easier to use the *elimination method* to find the point of intersection.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "efdf9704-19aa-5a4a-8ac3-220f267bc227" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9140 :tutorial/title "Lines Given in Standard Form"}]}
